// The fifth warning, and the only one raised while EMITTING C rather than while
// checking types -- which is why it needs a fixture of its own: an interpreted
// run never reaches the C back end, so `algc spec/warning.a24` could not
// provoke it however the program were written.
//
// It is the back end that had the problem.  The interpreter raises the moment
// the line runs and the bytecode machine refuses by name; only the C back end
// emitted a silent stub, so a program compiled, linked, passed every gate and
// aborted on the first run that reached the call.
//
// ⚠️ NOTHING ABOVE THIS MAY MOVE: the [WARN] line ERR-010 quotes carries this
// line number.
//
// Guarded so the emitted program still runs: the call is never reached, which
// is exactly the shape that made the fault outlive every test.
if False then WriteLn (NoSuchName ());
